EXPERIMENT NO. 7: REGISTERING OBSTACLES USING ROBOTSIM

1. AIM

To write a Java program to register obstacles of different shapes and colours in the RobotSim simulation environment and detect collisions using a touch sensor.

2. EXECUTION CODE

File Name: regobst.java

import ch.aplu.robotsim.*;
import ch.aplu.jgamegrid.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class regobst
{
    public regobst()
    {
        LegoRobot robot = new LegoRobot();
        Gear gear = new Gear();
        TouchSensor ts = new TouchSensor(SensorPort.S3);

        robot.addPart(gear);
        robot.addPart(ts);

        gear.setSpeed(30);
        gear.forward();

        while (true)
        {
            if (ts.isPressed())
            {
                gear.backward(1200);
                gear.left(750);
                gear.forward();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        new regobst();
    }

    private static GGBitmap bar(int width, int length, Color color)
    {
        GGBitmap bm = new GGBitmap(width, length);
        bm.setPaintColor(color);

        bm.fillRectangle(
            new Point(0, 0),
            new Point(width - 1, length - 1)
        );

        return bm;
    }

    private static GGBitmap circle(int radius, Color color)
    {
        GGBitmap bm = new GGBitmap(2 * radius, 2 * radius);
        bm.setPaintColor(color);
        bm.setLineWidth(3);

        bm.drawCircle(new Point(radius, radius), radius - 1);

        return bm;
    }

    static
    {
        RobotContext.setStartPosition(300, 200);
        RobotContext.setStartDirection(30);
        RobotContext.showNavigationBar();

        RobotContext.useObstacle(bar(300, 20, Color.red), 250, 150);
        RobotContext.useObstacle(bar(300, 20, Color.green), 250, 350);
        RobotContext.useObstacle(bar(20, 300, Color.blue), 150, 250);
        RobotContext.useObstacle(bar(20, 300, Color.yellow), 350, 250);
        RobotContext.useObstacle(circle(20, Color.black), 250, 250);
    }
}

3. THEORY

Obstacle registration is an important concept in robotics simulation. It involves creating obstacles in a virtual environment so that a robot can detect collisions and change its movement accordingly.

In this experiment, the RobotSim library is used to create a LegoRobot equipped with a Gear component and a TouchSensor connected to sensor port S3. The Gear controls the robot's movement, while the touch sensor detects physical contact with obstacles.

The RobotContext class configures the simulation environment. The setStartPosition() method specifies the robot's initial position, setStartDirection() sets its initial direction, and showNavigationBar() displays the simulation navigation bar.

The useObstacle() method registers obstacles at specified coordinates. The program defines two helper methods:

1. bar(): Creates a rectangular obstacle with a specified width, length, and colour using GGBitmap.

2. circle(): Creates a circular outline with a specified radius and colour using GGBitmap.

The program registers five obstacles: red, green, blue, and yellow rectangular bars, along with a black circle. Each obstacle is placed at a particular position on the simulation field.

The gear.setSpeed(30) method sets the robot's movement speed, and gear.forward() starts forward movement. The while loop continuously checks whether the touch sensor is pressed using ts.isPressed().

When the sensor detects an obstacle, the robot moves backward for 1200 milliseconds, turns left for 750 milliseconds, and resumes forward movement. This allows the robot to respond to collisions and attempt to avoid obstacles.

The experiment demonstrates obstacle registration, touch-sensor operation, collision detection, and basic obstacle-avoidance behaviour in a simulated environment.

4. CONCLUSION

The practical demonstrates how to register obstacles of different shapes and colours using RobotSim and JGameGrid. A touch sensor detects collisions, allowing the robot to move backward, turn left, and continue forward. This experiment provides an understanding of obstacle registration, collision detection, and sensor-based robot navigation.
